WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:07.090
- In a world where cat videos
and memes rule the feeds,

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.340
one media company will
rise above the noise,

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:15.135
this is Great Big Story.

00:00:15.135 --> 00:00:16.730
- [Director] Okay, Redd, that was great.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:17.650
Thank you.
- You got it.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:18.740
- Yeah, that was great.
- Cool.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:20.415
- [Director] You can stop
speaking like that now though.

00:00:20.415 --> 00:00:21.910
- What do you mean?

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:23.686
This is how I always speak.

00:00:23.686 --> 00:00:24.836
- [Director] Okay, cut!

00:00:28.098 --> 00:00:30.265
(humming)

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:35.400
- My name is Redd Pepper,
I'm a voiceover artist.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.448
In the UK, I voice
hundreds of movie trailers,

00:00:39.448 --> 00:00:41.940
"Men in Black," saving the Earth

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.890
from the scum of the universe.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.280
"Blair Witch Project," "Armageddon,"

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.100
"Space Jam," "Mr. Bean's Holiday,"

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.320
so many, I forget half of
them, to be honest with you.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.600
I started doing television adverts,

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.604
animations, audio books, I do
a lot of video games as well

00:00:59.604 --> 00:01:01.350
and a lot of them are sound effects,

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.482
a goose in the background.

00:01:03.482 --> 00:01:05.714
(donkey braying)

00:01:05.714 --> 00:01:07.270
(roaring)

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:08.500
Mr. Bean.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.600
When I first started doing movie trailers,

00:01:11.502 --> 00:01:12.950
that was fun:

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:17.030
one man, coming soon to a cinema near you.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:20.662
Sometimes I do romantic
movies . . . in a sleepy town;

00:01:20.662 --> 00:01:24.513
sometimes I'm doing horror
. . . don't answer the door!

00:01:24.513 --> 00:01:25.674
You've got to use your voice;

00:01:25.674 --> 00:01:27.288
you've got to raise it sometimes;

00:01:27.288 --> 00:01:29.670
and you've gotta take it to the depths.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:31.580
Very occasionally I get recognized,

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.710
but generally no, but the
time I do get recognized,

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.930
the phone goes off on
the train, guaranteed.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:41.043
Hello, and people look up
from behind the newspapers.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:46.030
The way I got into being a voice
artist was kind of strange,

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:48.680
I used to drive trains on
the London Underground;

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.330
one morning I was making my announcements,

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.810
all stations to Harrow, mind the doors.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:58.150
A television executive was
a passenger on my train;

00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:59.750
he got off at the next stop,

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.500
ran up to my cab, we exchanged details;

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.053
and the rest is history.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.629
I've had some strange
experiences as a voice artist,

00:02:08.629 --> 00:02:13.110
I was doing a trailer for
"Jurassic Park, The Lost World,"

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.238
Steven Spielberg movie and, kindly,

00:02:15.238 --> 00:02:18.370
they chose me to do the voice in the UK.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:19.743
Something is coming,

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:22.976
something big!

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:26.677
And as I said that, a voice
in my headphones said,

00:02:26.677 --> 00:02:28.570
"Wow, that's a great voice!"

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:32.360
And I didn't recognize
it was Steven Spielberg,

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.550
he was listening in from
the States into London.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.230
Well I swore, I said, who
the (expletive) is that?

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:39.250
Everybody went crazy in the studio,

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.637
"Shh, shh, no, it's Spielberg on the set!"

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:43.840
I appreciate what I do;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:45.630
I'm still meticulous about what I do;

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:47.300
I'm still proud of what I do.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.190
I really don't look at it as a job.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:53.671
I'm having fun. It really is a cool job;

00:02:53.671 --> 00:02:54.990
it's gotta be up there

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:57.606
with one of the coolest
jobs on the planet.

00:02:57.606 --> 00:02:59.070
Right, you got it?

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.293
Cool, I'm out of here.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.603
Oh, that's a wrap.

